Unified Cognitive-Engineering Architecture: Knowledge & Memory Subsystem
1. Executive Summary & Root Cause of Inconsistencies
The divergence between System A (4-Tier) and System B (5-Tier) stems from two architectural conflations:
1. Procedural vs. Episodic Conflation (System A, Tier 4):
   * System A combined executable logic with telemetry into a single layer (/automation_scripts/).
   * In cognitive architecture, Procedural Memory (executable skills/routines) must remain strictly decoupled from Episodic Memory (time-indexed trajectory logs and verifier outcomes).
2. Metacognitive Indexing vs. Procedural Skills (System A, Tier 3 vs. System B, Tier 5):
   * System A merged "The Almanac" (the global index/manifest) with "Skill Definitions" under /inference_skills/.
   * System B correctly separated the global directory (MAP.md / manifest.jsonl) as Tier 5, but omitted explicit prompt-level skill definitions and episodic run logs.
3. Retrieval Caching vs. Conceptual Knowledge (System B, Tier 3):
   * System B recognized that human-readable markdown (/wiki_md/) cannot be ingested at high velocity by LLM runtime workers, creating /recall_jsonl/ as an optimized machine-cache layer.
